  "notes": "Conventions and judgement calls. (1) 'is a predator' = primarily eats other animals, including insectivores, sanguivores and filter-feeders on animal plankton (humpback whale, sockeye salmon = true). Omnivores are false on BOTH 'is a predator' and 'is a strict herbivore' (domestic pig, raccoon, ostrich, mallard, common raven, domestic chicken, goldfish, ocellaris clownfish, ruby-throated hummingbird, American cockroach, red imported fire ant, earthworm), so the two diet attributes are not complements. (2) 'has fur or hair' is true for all mammals, taking the standard mammalian definition; cetacean hair is sparse or vestigial (humpback whales retain sensory vibrissae in tubercles; bottlenose dolphins have hairs only around birth). (3) 'has an exoskeleton' means a jointed chitinous arthropod cuticle. The garden snail's calcareous shell and the box jellyfish/octopus/earthworm bodies are false; some texts loosely call a mollusc shell an exoskeleton. (4) 'lives primarily in water' is true for semi-aquatic species that forage in water and only rest ashore: hippopotamus (in water by day, grazes on land at night), platypus, saltwater crocodile, sea otter, adult American bullfrog, and emperor penguin (most of the year at sea, breeding colony on ice). Canada goose is FALSE: it grazes largely on land, unlike the dabbling mallard, which is true. (5) 'can fly as an adult' means sustained powered flight. Domestic chicken is false (brief flutters only); domestic silkmoth is false (flightless through domestication); red imported fire ant is false (workers are wingless; only reproductives have a nuptial flight); American cockroach and dragonfly are true. (6) 'is domesticated' includes managed/husbanded species: honeybee, domestic silkmoth, reindeer (the only widely domesticated deer, Saami herding), and goldfish. Ostrich is false (farmed but not domesticated). (7) 'is venomous' means it produces venom delivered by a wound, not merely poisonous-if-eaten. True for male platypus spur, king cobra, western diamondback rattlesnake, reef stonefish, honeybee, black widow spider, emperor scorpion, box jellyfish, red imported fire ant, and common octopus (all octopuses produce venom via the salivary glands, though O. vulgaris is harmless to humans). (8) 'is primarily nocturnal' is true for species whose main activity/hunting period is night, including crepuscular-to-nocturnal ones: lion, gray wolf, domestic cat, hippopotamus, koala, saltwater crocodile, western diamondback rattlesnake (in the hot season). Polar bear, king cobra and giant-diurnal species are false. (9) 'weighs over 50 kg as an adult' uses typical adult body mass: domestic pig true (~100-150 kg), reindeer true (~90-130 kg), emperor penguin false (~23-40 kg), gray wolf false (~30-45 kg), sea otter false (~20-35 kg), domestic dog false (typical breeds well under 50 kg), Atlantic bluefin tuna and green sea turtle true. (10) 'lays eggs' is false for great white shark (ovoviviparous, live birth) and emperor scorpion (viviparous), true for platypus. (11) 'migrates long distances seasonally': monarch butterfly refers to the eastern North American population; sockeye salmon counts its anadromous spawning migration; plains zebra refers to the documented Serengeti/Botswana migrations.",
